ee  standard deviations so in the box in the  upper left corner you have a run with  your control that's run and it gives you  a number in this case that is greater  than two standard deviations above the  expected mean that is the one s it's  violating that one 2's rule which is  it's a single value that's two US two  standard deviations above the mean  so that would trigger a warning it's a  yellow flag saying there might be  something going on you need to pay  attention but it wouldn't automatically  fail your run the upper right corner is  an example of a violation that would  fail a run this is one where that  control value was three standard  deviations or more above outside of the  mean that would constitute a failure and  would require some sort of intervention  depending on what your lab procedures  are another example of a violation would  be in the bottom left which is a two two  a stroll that means that you've had two  consecutive values for that control that  test at greater than two standard  deviations above our outside of the mean  and then the bottom right is for one s  rule so four in a row that are at least  one standard deviation outside of the  mean so these may or may not constitute  failures of your run but they certainly  would require some sort of intervention  and review just to determine what's  going on  these are the types of rules that can be  applied to quantitative assays and these  are the types of rules that we do apply  in our evaluation of QC data and our  laboratory the way the approach that we  take is for our viral load assays and  particularly we're going to talk mostly  about HIV viral loads from our lab today  but in general our assays have for these  quantitative tests negative controls low  positive controls and high positive  controls we historically had third party  external controls and or in-house  developed and external controls that  were used in our laboratory developed  tests as an extra run control and really  it 